What activities fall under software engineering management according to SWEBOK v4.0a, and does that establish universal consensus?

Use these exact pinned knowledge graph excerpts and say what they do not prove:
{"claim":{"id":"se:CLM-07G-SWEBOK-SEM-DEFINITION","statement":"SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA9 defines software engineering management as the collection of work activities involved with planning, estimating, measuring, controlling, coordinating, leading and managing risk factors for a software project so that software products and engineering services are delivered efficiently, effectively and to the stakeholders' benefit, and treats project management and measurement management together in one knowledge area because of their close relationship.","scope":"SWEBOK Guide v4.0a Chapter 9 Introduction (printed pages 9-1 and 9-4); a professional-BOK definition claim, not a regulatory definition.","limitations":"The definition is the Guide's; adopting it here does not assert industry-wide terminological consensus."},"evidence":{"id":"se:EVD-07G-SWEBOK-SEM-INTRO","statement":"SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA9 defines software engineering management as the collection of work activities involved with planning, estimating, measuring, controlling, coordinating, leading and managing risk factors for a software project so that software products and engineering services are delivered efficiently, effectively and to the stakeholders' benefit, and treats project management and measurement management together because of their close relationship. The introduction asserts that measurement-informed management is a basic principle of any true engineering discipline — that management without measurement (qualitative and quantitative) suggests a lack of discipline and measurement without management suggests a lack of purpose or context — and adopts working definitions of management (a system of processes and controls to achieve the organization's strategic objectives) and measurement (assigning values and labels to work products, processes, resources and models derived from them). The chapter's breakdown is activity-based, listing seven topics (initiation and scope definition, project planning, project enactment, review and evaluation, closure, measurement, management tools) regardless of SDLC model.","limitations":["The Guide's authority limitations apply: generally accepted knowledge, not a uniform prescription.","The introduction cites an external perception that software is often delivered late, over budget and of poor quality; this lane does not adopt that perception as an empirical finding."]},"locator":{"id":"se:LOC-07G-SWEBOK-KA9-INTRO","section":"SWEBOK Guide v4.0a, Chapter 9 Software Engineering Management, Introduction (software engineering management definition; measurement-informed management discussion; working definitions of management and measurement; seven-topic activity-based breakdown incl. Figure 9.1); PDF page indices 201-206; printed pages 9-1 to 9-6"},"source":{"id":"se:SRC-SWEBOK-GUIDE-4-0A","title":"Guide to the Software Engineering Body of Knowledge (SWEBOK Guide), Version 4.0a","limitations":["A guide to the body of knowledge, not the complete body of knowledge","Generally accepted knowledge is not a uniform prescription for every project","Its topic citations are not comprehensive","Related disciplines are identified but not characterized in full"]}}
